section.section_3_2
    h2 The Man Who Would Be Stalin
    p
        |More than three decades ago, I turned from deep investigations of Georgian history to a serious study of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plGe") Georgia’s
        |&#32;
        |most famous and notorious son,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Ioseb Jughashvili
        |, the man who became
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |. There were already dozens of biographies of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |, and the need for another was disputable. But my interest was in his early life, his trajectory from the provincial town of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plGo") Gori
        |&#32;
        |where he was born to the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="OctR") October Revolution
        |.
    p
        |This was the least explored part of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |&#32;
        |life, even though he had lived as long before the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") revolution
        |&#32;
        |as he did after. When asked by a popular biographer who was plowing the same ground what I was focusing on, I answered that my interest was in the revolutionary movement, Social Democracy, labor, and Marxist thought and politics. “Oh good,” he said with relief, “I am interested in his women.”
    p
        |I set out to place
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |within the competing cultures through which the son of an alcoholic shoemaker became a major revolutionary and the architect of what he proclaimed to be the first “socialist” society in history. Toward the end of his life, he was arguably the most powerful individual in the world. Yet at various earlier times, he had been seen as a “gray blur,” “the man who missed the revolution,” a “mediocrity” hooked by history. He was
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="VlLe") Lenin’s
        |&#32;
        |“marvelous Georgian,” in wartime the “Generalissimo,” and still later “History’s villain.”
    p
        |Eschewing the methods of psychohistory, I nevertheless needed to probe into a man, his mind, and experience, all of which were singularly opaque.
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |is without doubt one of the most tempting and, at the same time, least hospitable subjects for a biographer. Not particularly introspective, he left few intimate letters, no secret diary, and many dubious witnesses to his inner life.
    p
        |Moreover,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Blsh") Bolshevik
        |&#32;
        |political culture was hostile to open personal expression and imposed on
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |and its other adherents an enforced modesty. Denial of the importance of self was part of the Social Democratic tradition. Even as a grotesque cult of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |&#32;
        |personality grew to gargantuan proportions,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |would continue, disingenuously, to claim that he disliked all the fuss.
    p
        |At different times in his life,
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |&#32;
        |created distinct mystifying narratives about who he was. In the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="date") 1930s
        |, that narrative drew parallels with
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="PeGr") Peter the Great
        |&#32;
        |and then with
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="IvTe") Ivan the Terrible
        |. His earlier narratives were romances about
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plGe") Georgia
        |, the revolutionary hero, the practical man of the underground, the hardened, steeled
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Blsh") Bolshevik
        |, who in time became the
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="person" data-id="VlLe") Lenin
        |&#32;
        |loyalist, the man of the moderate middle, and soon afterwards the radical transformer of
        |&#32;
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plRu") Russia’s
        |&#32;
        |reality.